---
// Examples style guide (PLAN.md §9): ClojureDocs' guide adapted to Python.
import Main from '../layouts/Main.astro';
import { highlightPython } from '../lib/highlight';

const code = (src: string) => highlightPython(src.trim());
---

<Main title="Examples Style Guide" description="How to write good Python examples for PyDocs." bodyClass="examples-styleguide-page">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="col-md-10 col-md-offset-1">
        <div class="row">
          <div class="col-md-10 col-md-offset-1 examples-styleguide-content">
            <section class="markdown">
              <h1>PyDocs Example Guidelines</h1>
              <p>
                Examples sit right under the docs for each function, class, and constant. Their job is to show how something is used, quickly
                and clearly. These guidelines help keep them that way.
              </p>

              <h2>General guidelines</h2>
              <p>Examples should be short, self-contained snippets that show one thing in the simplest way that works.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Decide what your example is about before you write it, and leave out everything else.</li>
                <li>Assume the reader knows programming but is new to Python.</li>
                <li>Short, complete, and correct beats clever.</li>
                <li>Standard library only: no third-party packages.</li>
                <li>It must run on the Python version these docs describe.</li>
              </ul>

              <h3>Include every import</h3>
              <p>A reader should be able to paste your example into a fresh interpreter and run it.</p>
              <p>Bad:</p>
              <pre class="syntaxify" set:html={code(`
Counter("mississippi").most_common(2)
# => [('i', 4), ('s', 4)]
`)} />
              <p>Good:</p>
              <pre class="syntaxify" set:html={code(`
from collections import Counter

Counter("mississippi").most_common(2)
# => [('i', 4), ('s', 4)]
`)} />

              <h3>Show results</h3>
              <p>
                Write the value of an expression in a <code># =&gt;</code> comment, and printed output in a <code># prints:</code> comment.
                The <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> interpreter style is fine too. Keeping to these forms means examples can be checked
                automatically later.
              </p>
              <pre class="syntaxify" set:html={code(`
import os.path

os.path.splitext("archive.tar.gz")
# => ('archive.tar', '.gz')

print(os.path.join("docs", "index.html"))
# prints: docs/index.html
`)} />
              <pre class="syntaxify" set:html={code(`
>>> "a,b,,c".split(",")
['a', 'b', '', 'c']
`)} />

              <h3>Broad or deep, not both</h3>
              <p>An example can show the range of inputs something accepts, like this one for <code>round</code>:</p>
              <pre class="syntaxify" set:html={code(`
round(2.675, 2)  # => 2.67 (floats are binary; 2.675 is stored as 2.67499...)
round(0.5)       # => 0
round(1.5)       # => 2   (ties round to the even number)
round(1234, -2)  # => 1200
`)} />
              <p>Or it can go deep on one use, like this one for <code>functools.lru_cache</code>:</p>
              <pre class="syntaxify" set:html={code(`
from functools import lru_cache

@lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def fib(n):
    return n if n < 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)

fib(80)
# => 23416728348467685

# Each value was computed once and then served from the cache.
fib.cache_info()
# => CacheInfo(hits=78, misses=81, maxsize=None, currsize=81)
`)} />
              <p>Please also point out any gotchas, about your example or about the function in general.</p>

              <h2>Comments</h2>
              <p>
                Use comments to explain what the next lines do, or to point out something a newcomer might miss. Don't repeat what the code
                already says.
              </p>
              <p>Bad:</p>
              <pre class="syntaxify" set:html={code(`
# call sorted on words
sorted(words, key=len)
`)} />
              <p>Good:</p>
              <pre class="syntaxify" set:html={code(`
words = ["banana", "fig", "apple"]

# Sort by length; sorted() is stable, so equal lengths keep their order.
sorted(words, key=len)
# => ['fig', 'apple', 'banana']
`)} />

              <h2>Formatting</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Follow <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/">PEP 8</a>. Indent with 4 spaces (the editor's Tab key inserts them).</li>
                <li>Keep lines under 80 characters. The editor shows a guide.</li>
                <li>Leave a blank line between separate ideas.</li>
              </ul>

              <h2>License</h2>
              <p>
                Examples, see-alsos, and notes on PyDocs are dedicated to the public domain under the
                <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">Creative Commons CC0 license</a>, so anyone can reuse them.
              </p>
            </section>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Main>
